# Test delta, the last layer of `.env -> .env.dev -> .env.test` (the harnesses
# set ENV_FILES=".env.dev:.env.test"). It carries ONLY the keys whose values
# differ from the layers beneath it; the Postgres-cluster block, DATABASE_PASSWORD,
# APP_DOMAIN, and the required-toggle dev values (e.g. GOOGLE_AUTH_PROVIDER=stub)
# are inherited from .env.dev.

# Tests must never depend on a hardcoded port being free, but the harness cannot
# simply export PORT: `.env` — the base layer, sourced first — assigns PORT,
# ADMIN_WEB_PORT and PUBLIC_WEB_PORT unconditionally, so any inherited value is
# destroyed before this file is reached. The harness (bin/scripts-tests)
# therefore exports BASE_TEST_PORT, a key `.env` does not own, and this layer
# re-derives every service port from it in every child that sources bin/common.
# BASE_TEST_PORT is the base of this checkout's 16-port block (bin/checkout-port,
# RFC 132); the offsets below are the registry that script's help text carries:
# +0 rest-server, +1 admin-web, +2 public-web for the TEST half of the block.
# 17984 is the fallback for tooling that sources .env.test without a harness
# having derived a block. It is 16-aligned and sits BELOW the 18000 block floor,
# so it is deliberately not a derivable block base: a port in the fallback range
# can never be mistaken for — or collide with — a block some checkout owns. (The
# previous 18080 was exactly block index 5.)
#
# The base is repeated in each expression rather than held in a scratch variable
# on purpose: these files are sourced under `set -a`, so a helper assignment
# would be EXPORTED into every child's environment.
PORT="${BASE_TEST_PORT:-17984}"
ADMIN_WEB_PORT=$(( ${BASE_TEST_PORT:-17984} + 1 ))
PUBLIC_WEB_PORT=$(( ${BASE_TEST_PORT:-17984} + 2 ))

# Per-worktree test DB so concurrent `bin/test` runs across worktrees never collide.
# PROJECT_ROOT is set by bin/common before this file is sourced; the value is
# exported and flows to the JVM via db.conf's ${POSTGRES_DB}.
# checkout_name, not a bare `basename`: the function carries the 63-byte guard
# (CHECKOUT_NAME_MAX_BYTES, bin/functions) that refuses a name PostgreSQL would
# silently truncate. `unicoach-test-` is the LONGEST of the three prefixes, so
# this line is precisely the one the budget is computed from — and it was the one
# with no guard at all, which is how a 64-byte test database name once shipped
# (RFC 174).
POSTGRES_DB=unicoach-test-$(checkout_name "$PROJECT_ROOT")

# --- Application Configuration (deltas vs .env) ---
# The JVM pool connects as this checkout's OWNER role, not the cluster superuser.
# `DATABASE_USER=postgres` meant every test run bypassed every ownership check
# from inside the application — the same hole as the shell half, one layer up
# (RFC 174). Dev is deliberately left alone: there DATABASE_USER=unicoach is a
# genuinely unprivileged app role, and exercising least privilege is the point.
#
# Written as a reference to POSTGRES_USER rather than a second copy of the
# derivation: .env.dev is layered BEFORE this file and owns the one definition of
# the role name.
DATABASE_USER=$POSTGRES_USER
DATABASE_MAXIMUM_POOL_SIZE=2
# Re-derived after PORT (which BASE_TEST_PORT may have overridden above).
SERVER_PORT=$PORT
